LONG_TEXT = """The history of the bicycle is a story of many small improvements made by people who rarely met one another. In 1817 Karl Drais, a forestry official in Baden, built a two-wheeled running machine with a steerable front wheel. Riders pushed themselves along with their feet, and for a short while the device was fashionable in Germany, France and England, where it was called the hobby horse or dandy horse. It was banned on pavements in several cities because riders frightened pedestrians, and the craze faded within a few years.

For decades afterwards, inventors experimented with three- and four-wheeled machines driven by levers and treadles. The next big step came in the 1860s in Paris, when pedals and cranks were attached directly to the front wheel. These velocipedes, built by makers such as Pierre Michaux and Pierre Lallement, had iron-rimmed wooden wheels and earned the nickname boneshakers. Even so, riding schools opened, races were organised, and magazines devoted to cycling appeared.

Because each turn of the pedals turned the front wheel exactly once, a larger front wheel meant a higher speed. Manufacturers in England, notably James Starley in Coventry, pushed this idea to its limit in the 1870s with the high-wheeler, later called the penny-farthing. Its front wheel could be as large as the rider's leg length allowed, often around 1.5 metres. Solid rubber tyres, wire spokes under tension and hollow steel tubing made these machines lighter and faster than anything before them. They were also dangerous: a rider sat high above the front wheel, and hitting a stone could throw them head first over the handlebars, an accident known as taking a header.

The safety bicycle solved this problem. In 1885 John Kemp Starley, James Starley's nephew, produced the Rover, which had two wheels of similar size and a chain driving the rear wheel. Gearing through the chain allowed a small wheel to travel as far per pedal stroke as a huge one. Within a few years the diamond-shaped frame became standard, and in 1888 John Boyd Dunlop, a Scottish veterinarian living in Belfast, developed a practical pneumatic tyre to give his son a smoother ride on a tricycle. Air-filled tyres made bicycles dramatically more comfortable and faster, and racers who adopted them began winning everything.

The 1890s saw a bicycle boom in Europe and North America. Prices fell as factories adopted mass-production methods, many of which were later used by the early car and aircraft industries. The Wright brothers ran a bicycle shop in Dayton, Ohio, and used their workshop skills to build their first aircraft. For women in particular, the bicycle offered a new kind of independent mobility. The American campaigner Susan B. Anthony said in 1896 that bicycling had done more to emancipate women than anything else in the world, and the need to ride comfortably helped change women's clothing, leading to bloomers and shorter skirts.

In the twentieth century, the bicycle's role diverged between countries. In much of North America it became mainly a children's toy and a sports machine as cars spread, while in the Netherlands, Denmark and parts of Asia it remained a primary means of everyday transport. Derailleur gears, which move the chain between sprockets of different sizes, became common in racing after the Tour de France allowed them in 1937. Later decades brought aluminium and carbon-fibre frames, mountain bikes developed by riders in California in the 1970s, and, more recently, electric-assist bicycles that use a small motor to support the rider's pedalling. Many cities now build protected cycle lanes and bike-sharing systems, treating the bicycle as a serious tool for reducing congestion and pollution.

Despite all these changes, a modern bicycle would be easily recognised by a rider from 1890: two wheels of equal size, a diamond frame, a chain to the rear wheel and air-filled tyres. Few machines have reached such a stable design so early and kept it for so long."""

